  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
         		 Look at the free-body diagrams on page 66 of the text. Why is the force <m>F_2</m> labeled like that
                 and not as <m>W_a</m>? Can you think of a situation where <m>F_2</m> and <m>W_a</m> 
                 have different magnitudes? Describe briefly.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	 On example 2.5.1, the book used the coordinate system shown on the left to describe the position of the
             block and wedge. Why not use the coordinate system on the right, which incorporates the 
             <q>constraint that the block slides on the wedge</q> more naturally?
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
            <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz3/wedge-conventional.svg">
                <shortdescription>Update Description</shortdescription>
            </image>

            <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz3/wedge-tilted.svg">
                <shortdescription>Update Description</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Draw the forces acting on the combined pulley-and-block system on example 2.5.2
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A table rests on the floor. A <alert>tightly stretched spring</alert> is connected between the 
                table and the floor, as shown below. Draw the force diagram for the table.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider a car at rest. Which of Newton's laws lets us conclude that the downward 
                gravitational pull of the Earth on the car and the upward contact force of the ground on the car 
                are equal and opposite?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>